WEBVTT

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:51.960
It began here in Ireland at the Harlan and Wolfe shipyard in Belfast.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:59.510
Three thousand men would labor here for more than two years.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.580
were building a monster. The largest ship the world had ever seen. In the spring of

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:21.400
1909, a mountain of steel began to rise against the sky. The ship would weigh 66,000 tons.

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:30.000
Her haul would span four city blocks. Each of her colossal steam engines was the size of a three

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:57.940
house. One of these things was a source of delight. It was a scene out of Gulliver's

00:01:57.940 --> 00:02:00.000
Travels when twenty draft horses were near

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.240
to haul the ship's anchor through the streets of Belfast.

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:13.400
Some few observers found this giant threatening

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:16.000
and wrote of her "nightmare scale."

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.620
But their forebodings fell short of the event,

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.330
for the fate of this ship still fascinates the world,

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:26.560
and her name is a synonym for tragedy.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:13.660
In 1910, the huge ship taking shape in Belfast was a supreme wonder in a world accustomed to miracles.

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:19.300
Every day, it seems, something bigger or better was invented.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:22.620
Never had so many people been so prosperous.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.340
Never had they taken such delight in showing off.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.000
So this was called the Gilded Age.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:44.850
this was a time when horses still got most people around

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:49.110
but things were rapidly changing thanks to the machines of a new age

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:56.090
everything from rubber bands to radios from light bulbs to automobiles

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.700
progress and prosperity

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:24.490
and machines. Almost anything seemed possible, and often it was. May 31st, 1911. The Royal

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:30.000
Mail Ship Titanic slipped gracefully into Belfast Harbor. It was the largest moving

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:39.130
object ever made by man.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:43.460
The Titanic was designed for the rich passenger trade on the North Atlantic.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:48.910
It was not only the biggest ocean liner, it was by far the most luxurious.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:59.220
Aboard Titanic, it was hard to remember that this was indeed a ship, advertising the delights

00:04:59.220 --> 00:04:59.920
it offered.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:04.260
The white star line called "Titanic" a floating palace.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:16.680
So confident were Titanic's builders that her trial voyage lasted just eight hours.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:20.960
Almost as an afterthought, she was said to be unsinkable.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:27.780
On April 10th, 1912, Titanic's maiden voyage began.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:41.450
With their maids, valets, and chauffeurs, their mountains of baggage, the rich travel in a style almost unknown today.

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:48.450
In an age that worshiped wealth, the 325 first-class passengers were an awesome assembly.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:56.500
Titanic was like a time capsule laden with the splendors of the Gilded Age.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:13.100
In 1912, these films were shown in theaters to a public eager for any glimpse of Titanic.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:18.520
In fact, this is actually Titanic's smaller sister ship, the Olympic.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:25.190
But the excitement and spectacle were true to the event, and many people couldn't tell the difference.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:40.290
Titanic sailed from South Hampton at noon. She was expected to reach New York in just

00:06:40.290 --> 00:06:53.500
seven days with 2,228 people aboard her. There are a few authentic pictures taken

00:06:53.500 --> 00:07:00.000
aboard Titanic on her first and last voyage. A vacation

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.900
The questioning priest, Fr. Francis Brown, caught these poignant snapshots of his fellow

00:07:04.900 --> 00:07:23.220
passengers, most of them on a voyage to eternity. The next day, Titanic made her last stop,

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:30.000
pausing off the coast at Queenstown, Ireland. Here, tenders brought out the

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:38.650
last passengers, mostly Irish immigrants headed for new homes in America. And here the lucky

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:53.430
Father Brown disembarked, taking these pictures on his way. Father Brown caught Captain Smith

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:58.710
peering down from Titanic's bridge, poised on the brink of destiny.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:05.750
Then Titanic sailed into the twilight zone of legend.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:11.020
She would not be photographed again for 73 years.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.620
Vanished in all but human memory.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:27.110
The events of Titanic's last hours have not faded with the passage of time.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:30.000
The tragedy, irony, and sheer terror.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:40.090
terror of this night still seize the imagination.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:45.400
A British film made in 1929 was one of the first of many Titanic movies.

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:24.560
And despite radio warnings, Titanic struck an iceberg.

00:09:24.560 --> 00:09:30.000
She carried only enough lifeboats for about 1,200 people and not even that many were saved.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:45.780
In 1986, a new chapter in the Titanic story began.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:50.260
The men and machines involved did not even exist when Titanic went down.

00:09:50.260 --> 00:10:00.000
From the Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution came the

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:11.020
submarine Alvin and Dr. Robert Ballard, a geologist and undersea explorer. For

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:16.450
decades Ballard had dreamed of being the man to explore the Titanic wreck. Now if

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:30.000
all goes well he may succeed within a few days. July 9th Ballard's expedition backed by the U.S.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:42.820
US Navy and drawing on proven underwater technology puts to sea from Woods Hole.

00:10:42.820 --> 00:10:52.710
The research vessel Atlantis II heads for Titanic's resting place, about 1,000 miles

00:10:52.710 --> 00:10:59.890
